CP                                                                    CP

NAME
    cp - copy files and directories, Unix-style

FORMAT
    cp [-fr] FROM ... TO

TEMPLATE
    none - flags are hand-parsed Unix bundles; `cp ?` prints usage

PATH
    C:cp

FUNCTION
    Copies files, pattern matches, and (with -r) whole directory
    trees. The last path is TO, everything before it is a source.
    With several sources or a pattern, TO must be an existing
    directory; a single plain source may name its copy directly.

    cp is non-destructive by default: an existing target file is
    SKIPPED and listed under "not copied:" at the end (return code
    5). -f deletes and replaces it instead - after checking with
    SameLock() that source and target are not the same object, so
    `cp -f file file` can never destroy the only copy. A directory
    is never replaced by a file.

    Metadata is preserved by default - protection bits, datestamp
    and filenote travel with the copy, the way Copy CLONE does.
    There is no -p flag: preserving is the sensible Amiga default.

OPTIONS
    -f  force: replace an existing target file (delete-then-copy,
        the Unix shape - if the copy itself then fails, the old
        target is already gone)
    -r  copy directories recursively (also accepted: -R)

RESULTS
    Return code 0 when clean, 5 when something was skipped (and
    listed), 10 when something failed, 20 on Ctrl-C. A broken or
    failed copy removes the partial target file. More than 32
    arguments is an error, never a silent truncation.

EXAMPLES
    cp file newname
    cp file work:archive/
    cp -r dir work:backup
    cp #?.txt ram:
    cp a b c work:stuff

NOTES
    -r walks the tree with an explicit work list, never native
    recursion, so a deep tree cannot blow the stack. Each directory
    is recreated at the destination, its files copied, its
    subdirectories queued; an existing target directory is merged
    into, files inside following the same skip/-f rule.

    Copying a directory into itself or its own subtree is refused
    ("cannot copy X into itself") - it would re-copy its own output
    until the disk filled.

SEE ALSO
    ls.doc, mv.doc, mkdir.doc, rm.doc, tools-audit.md
